// A Level Chemistry lessons for Lower Sixth (MINESEC LSS topics 1 to 7).
// Original text written for this app. **double asterisks** mark key terms
// (rendered bold). `examples` are worked examples.

const L = (id, unit, n, title, minutes, tags, body, figure, tip, check, terms, examples) => ({
  id,
  unit,
  n,
  title,
  minutes,
  paper: 'Paper 1 and Paper 2',
  tags,
  body,
  figure,
  ...(examples ? { examples } : {}),
  tip,
  check: { q: check[0], a: check[1], why: check[2] },
  terms,
});

export const LESSONS_L6 = [
  // ---------- 1. The mole concept ----------
  L('ac-mole-1', 'ac-mole', '1.1', 'Relative masses, the mole and chemical formulae', 12, ['Mole concept'], [
    'Atoms are far too small to count or weigh one by one, so chemists compare masses with a standard. The **relative atomic mass**, Ar, of an element is the average mass of its atoms compared with **one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom**. The **relative molecular mass**, Mr, is the sum of the Ar values of the atoms in a molecule; for ionic compounds the same sum is called the relative formula mass.',
    'The **mole** is the amount of substance containing as many particles as there are atoms in exactly 12 g of carbon-12. That number is the **Avogadro constant**, L = **6.02 × 10²³ mol⁻¹**. The mass of one mole, the **molar mass** M, is the Mr expressed in g/mol. So **n = m ÷ M**, and the number of particles is **N = n × L**.',
    'The **empirical formula** is the simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound. It is found from masses or percentages: divide each by the element’s Ar to get moles, then divide by the smallest number of moles. If a ratio is close to 1.5 or 1.33, multiply all values by 2 or 3.',
    'The **molecular formula** gives the actual numbers of atoms. It is a whole-number multiple of the empirical formula: divide the Mr by the empirical formula mass to find the multiple. The **percentage by mass** of an element in a compound is (number of atoms × Ar ÷ Mr) × 100.',
  ], 'mole-map', 'Set out empirical formula work as a table with columns: element, mass or %, ÷ Ar, ÷ smallest, ratio. It earns method marks even after a slip.',
  ['How many atoms are in 0.50 mol of water molecules?', ['9.0 × 10²³', '3.0 × 10²³', '6.0 × 10²³', '1.5 × 10²³'], 'Molecules = 0.50 × 6.02 × 10²³ = 3.01 × 10²³; each has 3 atoms, giving 9.0 × 10²³.'],
  [['Relative atomic mass', 'The average mass of an atom of an element relative to one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom.'], ['Mole', 'The amount of substance containing 6.02 × 10²³ particles.'], ['Empirical formula', 'The simplest whole-number ratio of atoms in a compound.']],
  [
    { q: 'A hydrocarbon contains 85.7% carbon by mass and has Mr = 56. Find its empirical and molecular formulae.', steps: ['Hydrogen = 100 − 85.7 = 14.3%.', 'Moles: C = 85.7 ÷ 12 = 7.14; H = 14.3 ÷ 1 = 14.3.', 'Divide by the smaller: C = 1, H = 2.0, so the empirical formula is **CH₂** (mass 14).', '56 ÷ 14 = 4, so the molecular formula is **C₄H₈**.'] },
    { q: 'Calculate the percentage of water of crystallisation in CuSO₄·5H₂O (Cu = 63.5, S = 32, O = 16, H = 1).', steps: ['Mr = 63.5 + 32 + 4(16) + 5(18) = 249.5.', 'Mass of water = 5 × 18 = 90.', 'Percentage = 90 ÷ 249.5 × 100 = **36.1%**.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-mole-2', 'ac-mole', '1.2', 'Reacting masses, yields and the gas laws', 13, ['Mole concept', 'Gases'], [
    'A balanced equation gives the **mole ratio** of reactants and products. To find a reacting mass: convert the given mass to moles, use the ratio from the equation, then convert back to mass. When the amounts of two reactants are given, find the **limiting reactant** first: it is the one that runs out, and it decides how much product forms.',
    'The **theoretical yield** is the mass of product calculated from the equation. The **percentage yield** = actual yield ÷ theoretical yield × 100. Yields are below 100% because of incomplete or reversible reactions, side reactions and losses during separation and purification. The **atom economy** = mass of desired product ÷ total mass of all products × 100 shows how much of the reactants ends up in useful product.',
    'For gases, Boyle’s law (pV constant at fixed T) and Charles’s law (V ∝ T at fixed p) combine into the **ideal gas equation, pV = nRT**, with p in Pa, V in m³, T in K and **R = 8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹**. Remember 1 dm³ = 10⁻³ m³ and 1 cm³ = 10⁻⁶ m³.',
    'By Avogadro’s law, equal volumes of gases at the same temperature and pressure contain equal numbers of molecules. One mole of any gas occupies **22.4 dm³ at s.t.p.** (273 K, 101 kPa) and about **24 dm³ at room temperature and pressure**. The equation pV = nRT also gives the molar mass of a volatile liquid: M = mRT ÷ pV. Real gases behave least ideally at high pressure and low temperature, when molecules are close and attract each other.',
  ], null, 'In pV = nRT the commonest mistakes are units: convert kPa to Pa, dm³ or cm³ to m³ and °C to K before substituting.',
  ['What volume does 0.25 mol of oxygen occupy at room temperature and pressure?', ['6.0 dm³', '24 dm³', '0.25 dm³', '96 dm³'], 'V = 0.25 × 24 = 6.0 dm³.'],
  [['Limiting reactant', 'The reactant that is completely used up and so limits the amount of product.'], ['Percentage yield', 'Actual yield divided by theoretical yield, times 100.'], ['Ideal gas equation', 'pV = nRT.']],
  [
    { q: 'What mass of calcium oxide is formed when 25.0 g of calcium carbonate is heated? CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ (Ca = 40, C = 12, O = 16)', steps: ['n(CaCO₃) = 25.0 ÷ 100 = 0.250 mol.', 'Mole ratio CaCO₃ : CaO = 1 : 1, so n(CaO) = 0.250 mol.', 'Mass = 0.250 × 56 = **14.0 g**.'] },
    { q: '0.204 g of a volatile liquid gives 80.0 cm³ of vapour at 373 K and 101 kPa. Find its Mr.', steps: ['p = 101 000 Pa, V = 80.0 × 10⁻⁶ m³, T = 373 K.', 'n = pV ÷ RT = (101 000 × 80.0 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ (8.31 × 373) = 2.61 × 10⁻³ mol.', 'M = m ÷ n = 0.204 ÷ 2.61 × 10⁻³ = **78.2 g/mol**, so Mr ≈ 78 (it could be benzene).'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-mole-3', 'ac-mole', '1.3', 'Solutions and titrations: acid-base and redox', 14, ['Mole concept', 'Titration', 'Practical'], [
    'The **concentration** of a solution is the amount of solute in each dm³: **c = n ÷ V** in mol/dm³ (mol dm⁻³), with V in dm³. Mass concentration (g/dm³) = c × M. A **standard solution** has an accurately known concentration: weigh the solid, dissolve it, transfer quantitatively to a volumetric flask with washings, make up to the mark and shake.',
    'In an **acid-base titration**, a pipette delivers a fixed volume of one solution and a burette adds the other until an indicator changes colour at the **end point**. Repeat until two **concordant** titres agree within 0.10 cm³ and average them. The calculation: moles of the burette solution from c × V, the mole ratio from the equation, then the unknown concentration.',
    '**Redox titrations** use the colour of the reagent. **Potassium manganate(VII)** in acid is purple and is reduced to almost colourless Mn²⁺, so it acts as its own indicator: the end point is the first permanent pale pink. MnO₄⁻ + 8H⁺ + 5Fe²⁺ → Mn²⁺ + 5Fe³⁺ + 4H₂O, a ratio of **1 : 5**. With ethanedioate ions the ratio is 2MnO₄⁻ : 5C₂O₄²⁻. **Iodine-thiosulphate** titrations use starch indicator: I₂ + 2S₂O₃²⁻ → 2I⁻ + S₄O₆²⁻.',
    'In a **back titration**, a known excess of reagent reacts with the sample (for example an insoluble carbonate such as limestone) and the excess left over is titrated. The amount that reacted = amount added − amount left over.',
  ], 'titration-apparatus', 'Write the mole ratio from the balanced equation on its own line before using it; most titration errors come from using 1 : 1 when it is not.',
  ['In acid solution, how many moles of Fe²⁺ react with 0.0020 mol of MnO₄⁻?', ['0.010 mol', '0.0020 mol', '0.0004 mol', '0.0050 mol'], 'The ratio MnO₄⁻ : Fe²⁺ is 1 : 5, so 5 × 0.0020.'],
  [['Concentration', 'The amount of solute in one dm³ of solution, in mol/dm³.'], ['Standard solution', 'A solution of accurately known concentration.'], ['Concordant titres', 'Titres that agree within 0.10 cm³.']],
  [
    { q: '25.0 cm³ of sodium hydroxide is neutralised by 21.6 cm³ of 0.100 mol/dm³ sulphuric acid. Find the concentration of the NaOH.', steps: ['n(H₂SO₄) = 0.100 × 21.6 ÷ 1000 = 2.16 × 10⁻³ mol.', '2NaOH + H₂SO₄ → Na₂SO₄ + 2H₂O, so n(NaOH) = 2 × 2.16 × 10⁻³ = 4.32 × 10⁻³ mol.', 'c = 4.32 × 10⁻³ ÷ 0.0250 = **0.173 mol/dm³**.'] },
    { q: 'An iron tablet is dissolved in acid and titrated with 0.0200 mol/dm³ KMnO₄; 12.5 cm³ is needed. Find the mass of iron in the tablet (Fe = 56).', steps: ['n(MnO₄⁻) = 0.0200 × 12.5 ÷ 1000 = 2.50 × 10⁻⁴ mol.', 'n(Fe²⁺) = 5 × 2.50 × 10⁻⁴ = 1.25 × 10⁻³ mol.', 'Mass = 1.25 × 10⁻³ × 56 = **0.070 g** (70 mg).'] },
  ]),

  // ---------- 2. Atomic structure ----------
  L('ac-atom-1', 'ac-atoms', '2.1', 'Sub-atomic particles, isotopes and the mass spectrometer', 12, ['Atomic structure'], [
    'Experiments revealed the parts of the atom. **Cathode rays** bent towards a positive plate and by magnets showed negative particles, **electrons**, present in all atoms. In the **Rutherford (gold foil) experiment**, most alpha particles passed straight through thin gold but a few bounced back, showing a tiny, dense, positive **nucleus**. **Protons** carry the positive charge; **neutrons**, discovered by Chadwick, are neutral and of similar mass.',
    'Relative masses and charges: proton 1, +1; neutron 1, 0; electron 1/1840, −1. In an electric field, protons and electrons are deflected in opposite directions (electrons much more, being lighter); neutrons are not deflected. **Isotopes** are atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons, so they have the same chemistry but different masses.',
    'The **mass spectrometer** measures the masses and abundances of isotopes. The sample is **vaporised**, **ionised** (by electron bombardment, forming mostly X⁺ ions), **accelerated** by an electric field, **deflected** by a magnetic field and **detected**. The deflection depends on the **mass-to-charge ratio (m/z)**: lighter ions are deflected more. The spectrum shows a peak for each isotope, its height proportional to abundance.',
    'Relative atomic mass from a mass spectrum: **Ar = Σ (isotopic mass × abundance) ÷ Σ abundance**. For molecules such as Cl₂, peaks appear at m/z 70, 72 and 74 in the ratio 9 : 6 : 1, from the combinations ³⁵Cl³⁵Cl, ³⁵Cl³⁷Cl and ³⁷Cl³⁷Cl.',
  ], 'atom-structure', 'List the five stages of a mass spectrometer in order and say what happens at each: vaporise, ionise, accelerate, deflect, detect.',
  ['Magnesium has isotopes ²⁴Mg (79%), ²⁵Mg (10%) and ²⁶Mg (11%). Its Ar is:', ['24.3', '25.0', '24.0', '24.8'], '(24 × 79 + 25 × 10 + 26 × 11) ÷ 100 = 24.32.'],
  [['Isotopes', 'Atoms with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.'], ['Mass-to-charge ratio', 'The quantity m/z that decides how far an ion is deflected in a mass spectrometer.'], ['Nucleus', 'The tiny, dense, positive centre of an atom.']],
  [
    { q: 'A sample of boron gives peaks at m/z 10 (height 23) and 11 (height 92). Calculate Ar of boron.', steps: ['Total abundance = 23 + 92 = 115.', 'Ar = (10 × 23 + 11 × 92) ÷ 115 = (230 + 1012) ÷ 115.', '= **10.8**.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-atom-2', 'ac-atoms', '2.2', 'Radioactivity and nuclear equations', 11, ['Atomic structure', 'Radioactivity'], [
    'Some nuclei are unstable and decay **spontaneously**, giving out radiation. **Alpha (α)** particles are helium nuclei, ⁴₂He: heavily ionising, stopped by paper, deflected slightly in fields. **Beta (β⁻)** particles are fast electrons from the nucleus (a neutron becomes a proton): stopped by a few mm of aluminium, deflected strongly. **Gamma (γ)** rays are electromagnetic waves: weakly ionising, very penetrating, reduced only by thick lead or concrete, and not deflected.',
    'Nuclear equations must balance in both **mass number** (top) and **atomic number** (bottom). Alpha decay: mass number −4, atomic number −2. Beta decay: mass number unchanged, atomic number +1. Gamma emission changes neither.',
    'The **half-life** is the time for half of the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay. It is constant for an isotope and unaffected by temperature or chemical state. After n half-lives, the fraction left is (½)ⁿ.',
    'Uses: **carbon-14** dating of once-living material (half-life 5730 years); **cobalt-60** gamma rays to treat cancer and sterilise medical equipment; **iodine-131** to study and treat the thyroid; tracers to find leaks in pipes; smoke detectors (americium-241). Radiation damages cells, so sources are handled with tongs, stored in lead and exposure time is kept short.',
  ], 'radiation-penetration', 'In nuclear equations, check the totals at the top and at the bottom separately; both must balance.',
  ['A sample has a half-life of 8 days. What fraction remains after 24 days?', ['1/8', '1/3', '1/4', '1/16'], '24 ÷ 8 = 3 half-lives; (½)³ = 1/8.'],
  [['Half-life', 'The time taken for half of the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay.'], ['Alpha particle', 'A helium nucleus, two protons and two neutrons.'], ['Beta particle', 'A fast electron emitted when a neutron in the nucleus becomes a proton.']],
  [
    { q: 'Thorium-234 (Z = 90) decays by beta emission. Write the equation and identify the product (Z = 91 is protactinium).', steps: ['Beta decay: mass number unchanged, atomic number increases by 1.', '²³⁴₉₀Th → ²³⁴₉₁Pa + ⁰₋₁e.', 'Check: top 234 = 234 + 0; bottom 90 = 91 − 1. The product is **protactinium-234**.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-atom-3', 'ac-atoms', '2.3', 'Energy levels, ionisation energies and electronic configuration', 15, ['Atomic structure', 'Electronic configuration'], [
    'When hydrogen gas is excited by an electric discharge, it emits light of only certain frequencies: a **line spectrum**. Each line comes from an electron falling from a higher **energy level** to a lower one, giving out a photon with energy **ΔE = hf**. Falls to n = 1 give the **Lyman series** (ultraviolet); falls to n = 2 give the **Balmer series** (visible). The lines get closer together at higher frequency and **converge**; the convergence limit of the Lyman series gives the **ionisation energy** of hydrogen. Line spectra prove that electrons have fixed (quantised) energies.',
    'The **first ionisation energy** is the energy needed to remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous atoms: X(g) → X⁺(g) + e⁻. It depends on **nuclear charge**, **distance** of the electron from the nucleus and **shielding** by inner electrons. **Successive ionisation energies** rise steadily, with a **large jump** when an electron is removed from a new, inner shell: sodium’s second IE (4563 kJ/mol) is about nine times its first (496 kJ/mol), showing it has one outer electron.',
    'Across a period the first IE generally rises, but with two dips that reveal **sub-shells**: it falls from Be to B (the B electron is in a 2p orbital, higher in energy) and from N to O (the O electron pairs up in a 2p orbital and is repelled by its partner). Down a group the first IE falls as the outer electron is further away and better shielded.',
    'Shells contain **sub-shells** (s, p, d, f) made of **orbitals**, regions where there is a high probability of finding an electron. Each orbital holds **two electrons of opposite spin** (Pauli). An s sub-shell has 1 orbital, p has 3, d has 5. Electrons fill orbitals in order of energy, **1s 2s 2p 3s 3p 4s 3d 4p** (the building-up or Aufbau principle), and occupy orbitals of the same sub-shell singly before pairing (Hund’s rule). Exceptions: chromium is [Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹ and copper is [Ar] 3d¹⁰ 4s¹. When transition metals form ions, the **4s electrons are lost first**.',
  ], 'hydrogen-spectrum', 'For questions on IE trends, always mention all three factors: nuclear charge, distance (shell or sub-shell) and shielding.',
  ['The electronic configuration of Fe²⁺ (Fe is Z = 26) is:', ['[Ar] 3d⁶', '[Ar] 3d⁴ 4s²', '[Ar] 3d⁵ 4s¹', '[Ar] 4s² 3d⁶'], 'Iron is [Ar] 3d⁶ 4s²; the 4s electrons are removed first.'],
  [['First ionisation energy', 'The energy to remove one mole of electrons from one mole of gaseous atoms.'], ['Orbital', 'A region around the nucleus that can hold up to two electrons of opposite spin.'], ['Line spectrum', 'Light of only certain frequencies, showing electrons have fixed energy levels.']],
  [
    { q: 'The successive ionisation energies of element X (kJ/mol) are 578, 1817, 2745, 11 578, 14 831. Which group is X in? Explain.', steps: ['The big jump is between the **third** and **fourth** IE (2745 to 11 578).', 'So three electrons are relatively easy to remove: they are in the **outer shell**; the fourth comes from an inner shell.', 'X is in **Group III** (it is aluminium).'] },
    { q: 'Write the full electronic configurations of S (Z = 16) and Cr (Z = 24).', steps: ['S: **1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁴**.', 'Cr: expected 3d⁴ 4s², but the half-filled d sub-shell is more stable: **1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s² 3p⁶ 3d⁵ 4s¹**.'] },
  ]),

  // ---------- 3. Bonding ----------
  L('ac-bond-1', 'ac-bonding', '3.1', 'Ionic, covalent, dative and metallic bonding', 13, ['Bonding'], [
    '**Ionic bonding** is the electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions in a giant lattice, formed by electron transfer from metal to non-metal atoms. Ionic compounds have high melting points, conduct when molten or dissolved, and are often soluble in water. Small, highly charged ions form stronger lattices.',
    '**Covalent bonding** is a shared pair of electrons attracted to both nuclei. Dot-and-cross diagrams show the shared pairs and the **lone pairs**. Some atoms expand their outer shell beyond eight (PCl₅, SF₆) and some have fewer than eight (BF₃). In a **dative (coordinate) bond**, both shared electrons come from one atom: NH₃ + H⁺ → NH₄⁺; in Al₂Cl₆, Cl lone pairs donate to Al.',
    '**Electronegativity** is the power of an atom to attract the bonding electrons in a covalent bond (Pauling scale: F 4.0, O 3.5, N 3.0, Cl 3.0, C 2.5, H 2.1). Unequal sharing gives a **polar bond** with δ+ and δ− ends. A molecule is **polar** only if its bond dipoles do not cancel: HCl and H₂O are polar, CO₂ and CCl₄ are not. Bonding forms a continuum: a small, highly charged cation **polarises** a large anion, giving ionic compounds some covalent character (Fajans’ rules), as in AlCl₃.',
    '**Metallic bonding** is the attraction between a lattice of positive metal ions and a sea of **delocalised electrons**. It explains conductivity (mobile electrons), malleability (layers of ions slide without breaking the bonding) and high melting points, which increase with the number of delocalised electrons (Na < Mg < Al).',
  ], 'ionic-bonding', 'When explaining a property, name the particles, the force between them and whether they can move: for example, "mobile delocalised electrons carry the current".',
  ['Which compound has the most covalent character?', ['AlI₃', 'NaF', 'NaCl', 'MgF₂'], 'Small, highly charged Al³⁺ strongly polarises the large I⁻ ion.'],
  [['Electronegativity', 'The power of an atom to attract the electrons in a covalent bond.'], ['Dative bond', 'A covalent bond in which both electrons come from the same atom.'], ['Metallic bond', 'Attraction between positive metal ions and delocalised electrons.']],
  [
    { q: 'Explain why magnesium has a higher melting point than sodium.', steps: ['Mg forms **Mg²⁺** ions and gives **two** delocalised electrons per atom; Na forms Na⁺ and gives one.', 'Mg²⁺ is also **smaller** than Na⁺.', 'So the attraction between the ions and the delocalised electrons is **stronger** in magnesium, and more energy is needed to melt it.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-bond-2', 'ac-bonding', '3.2', 'Shapes of molecules, sigma and pi bonds, hybridisation', 14, ['Bonding', 'Shapes'], [
    'The **valence shell electron pair repulsion (VSEPR)** theory states that electron pairs around a central atom repel each other and arrange themselves as far apart as possible. Repulsion increases in the order **bonding pair-bonding pair < lone pair-bonding pair < lone pair-lone pair**. A double bond counts as one region of electron density.',
    'Shapes: 2 pairs, **linear**, 180° (BeCl₂, CO₂); 3 pairs, **trigonal planar**, 120° (BF₃); 4 pairs, **tetrahedral**, 109.5° (CH₄); 3 bonding + 1 lone pair, **trigonal pyramidal**, 107° (NH₃); 2 bonding + 2 lone pairs, **bent**, 104.5° (H₂O); 5 pairs, **trigonal bipyramidal**, 90° and 120° (PCl₅); 6 pairs, **octahedral**, 90° (SF₆). Each lone pair closes the remaining bond angles by about 2.5°.',
    'Covalent bonds form by **overlap of orbitals**. End-on overlap gives a **sigma (σ) bond**, with electron density along the line between the nuclei; every single bond is a σ bond. Sideways overlap of two p orbitals gives a **pi (π) bond**, with electron density above and below that line. A double bond is σ + π; a triple bond is σ + 2π. π bonds are weaker and more exposed, which is why alkenes are reactive, and they prevent rotation.',
    '**Hybridisation** explains the shapes. Carbon in methane mixes one 2s and three 2p orbitals into four equivalent **sp³** orbitals at 109.5°. In ethene each carbon is **sp²** (three orbitals at 120°, in a plane) with one unhybridised p orbital forming the π bond. In ethyne each carbon is **sp** (two orbitals at 180°) with two p orbitals forming two π bonds.',
  ], 'vsepr-shapes', 'To predict a shape: count the bonding pairs and lone pairs on the central atom, name the electron-pair arrangement, then name the shape of the atoms only.',
  ['The shape of SF₆ is:', ['Octahedral', 'Tetrahedral', 'Trigonal bipyramidal', 'Square planar'], 'Six bonding pairs around sulphur, no lone pairs, all angles 90°.'],
  [['VSEPR', 'The theory that electron pairs around a central atom repel to be as far apart as possible.'], ['Sigma bond', 'A bond formed by end-on overlap of orbitals.'], ['Pi bond', 'A bond formed by sideways overlap of p orbitals, above and below the bond axis.']],
  [
    { q: 'Predict the shape and bond angle of PH₃ and of the NH₄⁺ ion.', steps: ['PH₃: P has five outer electrons. Three form bonds with hydrogen and two make a **lone pair**: 3 bonding pairs + 1 lone pair.', 'So PH₃ is **trigonal pyramidal**. VSEPR predicts about **107°**; the measured angle is smaller, about 93°.', 'NH₄⁺: four bonding pairs and no lone pair, so it is **tetrahedral, 109.5°**.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-bond-3', 'ac-bonding', '3.3', 'Intermolecular forces and crystal structures', 14, ['Intermolecular forces', 'Solids'], [
    '**Intermolecular forces** act between molecules and are much weaker than covalent bonds. **Van der Waals (London dispersion) forces** exist between all molecules: moving electrons create **temporary dipoles** that induce dipoles in neighbours. They increase with the **number of electrons** (bigger molecules) and with **surface contact** (straight-chain alkanes boil higher than branched isomers).',
    '**Permanent dipole-dipole forces** act between polar molecules such as HCl and propanone, in addition to dispersion forces. **Hydrogen bonds** are the strongest: they form between a hydrogen atom bonded to **N, O or F** and a lone pair on another N, O or F. They explain the high boiling points of H₂O, NH₃ and HF compared with the other hydrides of their groups, the solubility of alcohols and sugars in water, the double helix of DNA, and why **ice is less dense than water**: the hydrogen-bonded open lattice holds molecules further apart.',
    'In crystals, particles are arranged in a regular repeating pattern. The smallest repeating unit is the **unit cell**. Metals adopt **body-centred cubic** (coordination number 8, e.g. sodium, iron), **face-centred cubic = cubic close packing** (12, e.g. copper, aluminium) or **hexagonal close packing** (12, e.g. magnesium, zinc). Close packing fills 74% of space.',
    'Ionic lattices depend on the radius ratio of the ions: **sodium chloride** has 6 : 6 coordination (each ion surrounded octahedrally by six oppositely charged ions); **caesium chloride**, with the larger Cs⁺, has 8 : 8 coordination. **X-ray diffraction** reveals these structures: the regular layers of particles diffract X-rays into a pattern from which the spacing of the layers and the positions of the particles are worked out.',
  ], 'giant-structures', 'Rank boiling points by first asking: can it hydrogen bond? Then: is it polar? Then compare the number of electrons.',
  ['Which has the highest boiling point?', ['Butan-1-ol', 'Pentane', 'Butanal', 'Diethyl ether'], 'Similar sizes, but only butan-1-ol forms hydrogen bonds between its molecules.'],
  [['Van der Waals forces', 'Weak attractions between molecules caused by temporary dipoles.'], ['Hydrogen bond', 'An attraction between H bonded to N, O or F and a lone pair on another N, O or F.'], ['Unit cell', 'The smallest repeating unit of a crystal lattice.']],
  [
    { q: 'Explain the trend in boiling points: CH₄ (−162 °C), SiH₄ (−112 °C), H₂O (100 °C), H₂S (−60 °C).', steps: ['CH₄ and SiH₄ are non-polar: only **dispersion forces**; SiH₄ has more electrons, so it boils higher.', 'H₂S has dispersion and dipole-dipole forces, but no hydrogen bonds.', 'H₂O forms **hydrogen bonds** between molecules, which are much stronger, so its boiling point is far higher than that of H₂S, even though H₂S has more electrons.'] },
  ]),

  // ---------- 4. Organic chemistry 1 ----------
  L('ac-org1-1', 'ac-organic1', '4.1', 'Organic fundamentals: series, naming and isomerism', 14, ['Organic', 'Isomerism'], [
    'Organic chemistry is the chemistry of carbon compounds. Carbon forms four strong covalent bonds and long chains and rings (**catenation**). A **homologous series** is a family with the same **functional group** and general formula, in which successive members differ by **CH₂** and show a gradual change in physical properties and similar chemical properties.',
    'Formulae: **molecular** (C₂H₆O), **structural** (CH₃CH₂OH), **displayed** (all bonds shown) and **skeletal** (carbon chain as zigzag lines, hydrogens on carbon omitted). Common functional groups: alkene C=C, alcohol –OH, halogenoalkane –X, aldehyde –CHO, ketone C–CO–C, carboxylic acid –COOH, ester –COO–, amine –NH₂, nitrile –CN, amide –CONH₂.',
    '**IUPAC naming**: find the longest carbon chain containing the functional group (meth-, eth-, prop-, but-, pent-, hex-); number from the end that gives the functional group the lowest number; name side chains (methyl, ethyl) with their positions; list substituents alphabetically; use di-, tri- for repeats. Examples: 2-methylbutane, but-1-ene, propan-2-ol, 2-chloropropane.',
    '**Isomers** have the same molecular formula but different arrangements. **Structural isomers** differ in chain (butane and 2-methylpropane), position (propan-1-ol and propan-2-ol) or functional group (ethanol and methoxymethane). **Stereoisomers** have the same structure but a different arrangement in space: **geometric (cis-trans)** isomers arise from restricted rotation about C=C when each carbon carries two different groups; **optical** isomers arise from a **chiral carbon** with four different groups and are non-superimposable mirror images (enantiomers) that rotate plane-polarised light in opposite directions.',
  ], 'organic-structures', 'For each isomer question, draw the structures before naming them; examiners mark the structure and the name separately.',
  ['Which compound shows optical isomerism?', ['Butan-2-ol', 'Propan-2-ol', 'Butan-1-ol', 'Ethanol'], 'In butan-2-ol, C2 carries H, OH, CH₃ and C₂H₅: four different groups.'],
  [['Homologous series', 'A family of compounds with the same functional group, differing by CH₂.'], ['Structural isomers', 'Compounds with the same molecular formula but different structural formulae.'], ['Chiral carbon', 'A carbon atom bonded to four different groups.']],
  [
    { q: 'Draw and name the structural isomers of C₄H₁₀ and the alkene isomers of C₄H₈ (ignore rings).', steps: ['C₄H₁₀: **butane** CH₃CH₂CH₂CH₃ and **2-methylpropane** CH₃CH(CH₃)CH₃.', 'C₄H₈ alkenes: **but-1-ene** CH₂=CHCH₂CH₃, **but-2-ene** CH₃CH=CHCH₃ and **2-methylpropene** CH₂=C(CH₃)₂.', 'But-2-ene also has **cis and trans** forms, giving four alkene isomers in all.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-org1-2', 'ac-organic1', '4.2', 'Purifying and analysing organic compounds', 12, ['Organic', 'Practical'], [
    'Organic products are rarely pure. Liquids are purified by **simple** or **fractional distillation**, by washing in a separating funnel (for example with sodium hydrogencarbonate solution to remove acid), drying with an anhydrous salt (calcium chloride or magnesium sulphate) and redistilling. **Steam distillation** separates volatile, water-immiscible substances such as essential oils.',
    'Solids are purified by **recrystallisation**: dissolve in the minimum of hot solvent, filter hot to remove insoluble impurities, cool to crystallise, filter under reduced pressure, wash with a little cold solvent and dry. **Chromatography** (paper, thin-layer, column) separates mixtures; the Rf value = distance moved by the spot ÷ distance moved by the solvent.',
    'Purity is tested by a **sharp melting point** that matches the data book value (impurities lower it and widen the range), a **constant boiling point**, and a **single spot** on a chromatogram.',
    'The elements present are found by tests. Carbon and hydrogen: heat with copper(II) oxide; CO₂ turns limewater milky and water turns anhydrous copper(II) sulphate blue. Nitrogen, sulphur and halogens: the **Lassaigne (sodium fusion) test** converts them to CN⁻, S²⁻ and X⁻, which are then identified. **Combustion analysis** gives the masses of CO₂ and H₂O formed from a known mass of compound, from which the empirical formula is calculated.',
  ], 'fractionating-column', 'In combustion analysis: mass of C = mass of CO₂ × 12/44; mass of H = mass of H₂O × 2/18; oxygen is found by difference.',
  ['A solid melts sharply at the value given in the data book. This shows it is:', ['Pure', 'Impure', 'A mixture of two compounds', 'Wet'], 'Impurities lower the melting point and spread it over a range.'],
  [['Recrystallisation', 'Purifying a solid by dissolving it in hot solvent and letting crystals form on cooling.'], ['Rf value', 'Distance moved by a substance divided by distance moved by the solvent.'], ['Combustion analysis', 'Finding an empirical formula from the masses of CO₂ and H₂O formed on burning.']],
  [
    { q: '0.230 g of an organic compound containing C, H and O burns to give 0.440 g CO₂ and 0.270 g H₂O. Find the empirical formula.', steps: ['C = 0.440 × 12/44 = 0.120 g; H = 0.270 × 2/18 = 0.030 g; O = 0.230 − 0.150 = 0.080 g.', 'Moles: C = 0.0100, H = 0.030, O = 0.0050.', 'Ratio ÷ 0.0050: C 2, H 6, O 1, so the empirical formula is **C₂H₆O**.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-org1-3', 'ac-organic1', '4.3', 'Petroleum and the alkanes', 12, ['Organic', 'Alkanes'], [
    '**Crude oil (petroleum)** is a mixture of hydrocarbons, mainly alkanes, cycloalkanes and arenes. It is separated by **fractional distillation** into fractions with different boiling ranges: refinery gas, petrol (gasoline), naphtha, kerosene, diesel, lubricating oil and bitumen. Cameroon refines crude oil at **SONARA** in Limbe.',
    'Demand for petrol and alkenes is greater than the supply from distillation. **Cracking** breaks long-chain alkanes into shorter alkanes and alkenes: **thermal cracking** uses high temperature and pressure; **catalytic cracking** uses zeolite catalysts at about 450 °C and gives branched alkanes and aromatics for petrol. **Reforming** converts straight-chain alkanes into branched, cyclic and aromatic hydrocarbons that burn more smoothly (higher octane number).',
    'Alkanes, CₙH₂ₙ₊₂, are **saturated** and fairly unreactive because C–C and C–H bonds are strong and non-polar. They burn completely to CO₂ and H₂O; in limited oxygen they give poisonous **carbon monoxide** and soot. Unburned hydrocarbons and nitrogen oxides from engines are removed by **catalytic converters** (platinum, rhodium).',
    'With chlorine or bromine in **ultraviolet light**, alkanes undergo **free-radical substitution**. **Initiation**: Cl₂ → 2Cl•. **Propagation**: Cl• + CH₄ → HCl + •CH₃, then •CH₃ + Cl₂ → CH₃Cl + Cl•. **Termination**: two radicals combine (Cl• + •CH₃ → CH₃Cl; 2•CH₃ → C₂H₆). Further substitution gives CH₂Cl₂, CHCl₃ and CCl₄, so a mixture forms.',
  ], 'fractionating-column', 'In the free-radical mechanism, label each step (initiation, propagation, termination) and show the unpaired electrons as dots.',
  ['Why does the reaction of methane with chlorine need ultraviolet light?', ['To split Cl₂ into chlorine radicals', 'To heat the methane', 'To make chlorine ions', 'To act as a catalyst for combustion'], 'UV provides the energy for homolytic fission of the Cl–Cl bond.'],
  [['Cracking', 'Breaking long-chain alkanes into shorter alkanes and alkenes.'], ['Free radical', 'A species with an unpaired electron.'], ['Saturated', 'Containing only single carbon-carbon bonds.']],
  [
    { q: 'Write an equation for the cracking of decane, C₁₀H₂₂, into octane and ethene.', steps: ['Start with C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + ?', 'Atoms left: C 10 − 8 = 2 and H 22 − 18 = 4, which is C₂H₄.', '**C₁₀H₂₂ → C₈H₁₈ + C₂H₄**.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-org1-4', 'ac-organic1', '4.4', 'Alkenes, alkynes and arenes', 14, ['Organic', 'Alkenes', 'Arenes'], [
    '**Alkenes**, CₙH₂ₙ, contain a C=C double bond. The π electrons form an electron-rich region that attracts **electrophiles**, so alkenes undergo **electrophilic addition**. With bromine (in water or an organic solvent), the orange colour disappears: this is the **test for C=C**. Other additions: H₂ with a nickel catalyst (hydrogenation, used to harden vegetable oils into margarine); HBr; steam with a phosphoric acid catalyst (making ethanol); cold acidified KMnO₄, which gives a diol and is decolourised.',
    'Mechanism with HBr: the π electrons attack the δ+ hydrogen, forming a **carbocation** and Br⁻; then Br⁻ attacks the carbocation. With unsymmetrical alkenes, **Markovnikov’s rule** applies: the hydrogen adds to the carbon that already has more hydrogens, because this produces the more stable (secondary or tertiary) carbocation, stabilised by the electron-releasing alkyl groups.',
    'Alkenes undergo **addition polymerisation**: many ethene molecules join to form poly(ethene); propene gives poly(propene) and chloroethene gives PVC. **Alkynes**, CₙH₂ₙ₋₂, such as ethyne, also add reagents, usually in two stages; ethyne burns with a very hot flame used in welding.',
    '**Benzene**, C₆H₆, is a planar hexagon in which each carbon contributes one p electron to a ring of **delocalised π electrons**. All C–C bonds are the same length, between single and double, and benzene is more stable than the Kekulé structure suggests (its enthalpy of hydrogenation is about 152 kJ/mol less exothermic than expected). It therefore undergoes **electrophilic substitution**, keeping the stable ring: **nitration** with concentrated HNO₃ and H₂SO₄ at about 50 °C (electrophile NO₂⁺); **halogenation** with Br₂ and an FeBr₃ (halogen carrier) catalyst; **Friedel-Crafts alkylation** with a chloroalkane and AlCl₃.',
  ], 'organic-structures', 'In mechanisms, curly arrows start from a bond or a lone pair and point to where the electrons go; show partial charges and the carbocation clearly.',
  ['Propene reacts with HBr. The major product is:', ['2-bromopropane', '1-bromopropane', '1,2-dibromopropane', 'Propane'], 'The secondary carbocation is more stable, so Br ends up on the middle carbon.'],
  [['Electrophile', 'A species that accepts a pair of electrons, attracted to electron-rich regions.'], ['Carbocation', 'An ion with a positively charged carbon atom.'], ['Delocalised electrons', 'Electrons spread over several atoms rather than held between two.']],
  [
    { q: 'Describe a test to distinguish cyclohexene from benzene.', steps: ['Add a few drops of **bromine water** to each and shake.', 'Cyclohexene **decolourises** the bromine at once (electrophilic addition across C=C).', 'Benzene does **not** decolourise it under these conditions, because addition would destroy the stable delocalised ring.'] },
  ]),

  // ---------- 5. Energetics ----------
  L('ac-energy-1', 'ac-energetics', '5.1', 'Enthalpy changes and calorimetry', 13, ['Energetics'], [
    'The **enthalpy change**, ΔH, is the heat energy exchanged with the surroundings at constant pressure. In an **exothermic** reaction heat is given out, the surroundings warm up and ΔH is **negative**; in an **endothermic** reaction heat is taken in and ΔH is **positive**. **Energy level (profile) diagrams** show reactants and products at different heights, with the activation energy as a hump.',
    '**Standard conditions** are 298 K and 100 kPa, with solutions at 1 mol/dm³ and substances in their standard states (symbol ⦵ or °). Key definitions: **standard enthalpy of formation**, ΔHf°, when one mole of a compound forms from its elements in their standard states; **standard enthalpy of combustion**, ΔHc°, when one mole burns completely in oxygen; **enthalpy of neutralisation**, when acid and alkali react to form one mole of water (about −57 kJ/mol for strong acids and strong bases).',
    '**Calorimetry** measures enthalpy changes. Heat exchanged **q = m c ΔT**, where m is the mass of water or solution heated, c its specific heat capacity (4.18 J g⁻¹ K⁻¹) and ΔT the temperature change. Then **ΔH = −q ÷ n**, where n is the moles of the limiting reactant (or of fuel burned), with the sign showing whether heat was given out.',
    'Results are usually less exothermic than data book values because of **heat loss** to the surroundings, incomplete combustion and evaporation of fuel. Insulation, a lid, a draught shield and extrapolating a temperature-time graph back to the moment of mixing improve accuracy.',
  ], 'energy-profile', 'Always give ΔH a sign and units, kJ/mol, and say per mole of what (for example, per mole of water formed).',
  ['When 0.010 mol of a fuel burns, it heats 100 g of water by 20 K. ΔHc is about:', ['−836 kJ/mol', '+836 kJ/mol', '−8.36 kJ/mol', '−83.6 kJ/mol'], 'q = 100 × 4.18 × 20 = 8360 J; ÷ 0.010 = 836 000 J/mol, negative because heat is released.'],
  [['Enthalpy change', 'The heat energy exchanged at constant pressure.'], ['Standard enthalpy of formation', 'The enthalpy change when one mole of a compound forms from its elements in their standard states.'], ['Exothermic', 'Giving out heat to the surroundings; ΔH negative.']],
  [
    { q: '50.0 cm³ of 1.00 mol/dm³ HCl and 50.0 cm³ of 1.00 mol/dm³ NaOH, both at 22.0 °C, are mixed. The temperature rises to 28.7 °C. Find the enthalpy of neutralisation.', steps: ['Mass of solution ≈ 100 g; ΔT = 6.7 K.', 'q = 100 × 4.18 × 6.7 = 2800 J = 2.80 kJ.', 'Moles of water formed = 0.0500.', 'ΔH = −2.80 ÷ 0.0500 = **−56 kJ/mol**.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-energy-2', 'ac-energetics', '5.2', 'Hess’s law and bond enthalpies', 14, ['Energetics', 'Hess'], [
    '**Hess’s law**: the enthalpy change of a reaction depends only on the initial and final states, not on the route. This lets us calculate enthalpy changes that cannot be measured directly, by building a **cycle** of reactions whose ΔH values are known.',
    'Using **enthalpies of formation**: **ΔH(reaction) = Σ ΔHf(products) − Σ ΔHf(reactants)** (elements have ΔHf = 0). Using **enthalpies of combustion**: **ΔH(reaction) = Σ ΔHc(reactants) − Σ ΔHc(products)**, the route going via the combustion products. This is how the enthalpy of formation of a hydrocarbon, which cannot be made directly from carbon and hydrogen, is found.',
    'A **bond enthalpy** is the energy needed to break one mole of a covalent bond in gaseous molecules. Bond breaking is endothermic; bond making is exothermic. **ΔH ≈ Σ (bonds broken) − Σ (bonds made)**. Mean bond enthalpies are averages over many compounds, so the answers are approximate and differ from values found from enthalpies of formation.',
    'Hess cycles must be balanced and include state symbols; multiply ΔH values by the number of moles used. Draw arrows clearly, and follow the direction of each arrow: going against an arrow changes the sign of that ΔH.',
  ], 'energy-profile', 'Write the cycle with the target reaction along the top, then add the two routes underneath. Label every arrow with its ΔH before calculating.',
  ['ΔHf of CO₂ is −394 and of H₂O(l) is −286 kJ/mol. ΔHc of hydrogen is:', ['−286 kJ/mol', '−394 kJ/mol', '−680 kJ/mol', '+286 kJ/mol'], 'Burning hydrogen is the same reaction as forming water from its elements.'],
  [['Hess’s law', 'The enthalpy change of a reaction is independent of the route taken.'], ['Bond enthalpy', 'The energy needed to break one mole of a bond in gaseous molecules.'], ['Enthalpy cycle', 'A diagram linking reactions so that an unknown ΔH can be calculated.']],
  [
    { q: 'Calculate ΔHf of methane from: ΔHc(C) = −394, ΔHc(H₂) = −286, ΔHc(CH₄) = −890 kJ/mol.', steps: ['Target: C(s) + 2H₂(g) → CH₄(g).', 'Using combustion: ΔHf = Σ ΔHc(reactants) − Σ ΔHc(products).', '= [−394 + 2(−286)] − (−890) = −966 + 890 = **−76 kJ/mol** (data book −75).'] },
    { q: 'Estimate ΔH for CH₂=CH₂ + H₂ → CH₃CH₃ using bond enthalpies (kJ/mol): C=C 612, H–H 436, C–C 348, C–H 413.', steps: ['Bonds broken: C=C + H–H = 612 + 436 = 1048.', 'Bonds made: C–C + 2 C–H (the other four C–H are unchanged) = 348 + 2(413) = 1174.', 'ΔH = 1048 − 1174 = **−126 kJ/mol**.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-energy-3', 'ac-energetics', '5.3', 'Born-Haber cycles, lattice and hydration enthalpies', 15, ['Energetics', 'Ionic compounds'], [
    'The **lattice enthalpy** (of formation) is the enthalpy change when one mole of an ionic solid forms from its gaseous ions: Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g) → NaCl(s), ΔH = −787 kJ/mol. It cannot be measured directly, so it is found from a **Born-Haber cycle**, a Hess cycle built from: enthalpy of **formation**, enthalpies of **atomisation** of each element, **ionisation energies** of the metal and **electron affinities** of the non-metal.',
    'The **first electron affinity** is the enthalpy change when each atom in a mole of gaseous atoms gains one electron: Cl(g) + e⁻ → Cl⁻(g), ΔH = −349 kJ/mol (exothermic). The **second electron affinity**, O⁻(g) + e⁻ → O²⁻(g), is **endothermic** (+798 kJ/mol), because an electron is being added to an ion that is already negative.',
    'Lattice enthalpy becomes more exothermic for ions with **higher charge** and **smaller radius**: MgO (−3791) ≫ NaCl (−787) > KCl (−718). A **theoretical** lattice enthalpy can be calculated assuming purely ionic, spherical ions. When the Born-Haber (experimental) value is noticeably more exothermic than the theoretical value, as for AgCl or AgI, the bonding has **covalent character** caused by polarisation of the anion.',
    'When an ionic solid dissolves, the lattice is broken (endothermic) and the ions are **hydrated** (exothermic: water molecules’ δ− oxygen surrounds cations and δ+ hydrogens surround anions). **ΔH(solution) = −ΔH(lattice formation) + Σ ΔH(hydration)**. Smaller, more highly charged ions have more exothermic hydration enthalpies. Fuels and energy sources compared on a large scale include fossil fuels, biomass, hydroelectricity and solar energy, with their effects on the environment.',
  ], 'born-haber', 'In a Born-Haber cycle, check the direction of every arrow and count atoms: half a mole of Cl₂ is atomised for NaCl, but a whole mole for MgCl₂.',
  ['Which ion has the most exothermic enthalpy of hydration?', ['Mg²⁺', 'Na⁺', 'K⁺', 'Ca²⁺'], 'It is small and doubly charged, so it attracts water molecules most strongly.'],
  [['Lattice enthalpy', 'The enthalpy change when one mole of an ionic solid forms from its gaseous ions.'], ['Electron affinity', 'The enthalpy change when each atom in a mole of gaseous atoms gains one electron.'], ['Hydration enthalpy', 'The enthalpy change when one mole of gaseous ions dissolves in water.']],
  [
    { q: 'Use the data (kJ/mol) to find the lattice enthalpy of NaCl: ΔHf(NaCl) −411; atomisation Na +107; IE₁(Na) +496; atomisation ½Cl₂ +122; EA(Cl) −349.', steps: ['By Hess’s law, ΔHf = sum of all the other steps + lattice enthalpy.', '−411 = 107 + 496 + 122 − 349 + LE = 376 + LE.', 'LE = −411 − 376 = **−787 kJ/mol**.'] },
    { q: 'The lattice enthalpy of KCl is −718 kJ/mol; hydration enthalpies are K⁺ −322 and Cl⁻ −364 kJ/mol. Find the enthalpy of solution.', steps: ['ΔH(solution) = −(lattice formation) + Σ hydration.', '= +718 + (−322 − 364) = 718 − 686.', '= **+32 kJ/mol**: endothermic, so the solution cools as KCl dissolves.'] },
  ]),

  // ---------- 6. Periodicity and the s-block ----------
  L('ac-period-1', 'ac-periodicity', '6.1', 'The modern Periodic Table and periodicity across Period 3', 14, ['Periodicity'], [
    'The modern Periodic Table arranges elements by **atomic number**. Elements are grouped into **blocks** by the sub-shell holding their outermost electrons: the **s-block** (Groups I and II), **p-block** (Groups III to 0), **d-block** (transition metals) and **f-block** (lanthanides and actinides). Metals lie on the left, non-metals on the right, and **metalloids** (B, Si, Ge, As) between them.',
    '**Periodicity** is the repeating pattern of properties across periods. Across Period 3 (Na to Ar): **atomic radius decreases** (rising nuclear charge, same shell); **first ionisation energy** generally increases (dips at Al and S); **electronegativity** increases (to Cl).',
    '**Melting points** follow structure: they rise from Na to Al (**metallic** bonding with more delocalised electrons), peak at **Si** (**giant covalent**), then are low for P₄, S₈ and Cl₂ (**simple molecules**: S₈ is highest of these, having the most electrons) and lowest for Ar (single atoms). Electrical conductivity is high for the metals, low for Si (a semiconductor) and nil for the rest.',
    '**Oxides**: Na₂O and MgO are **ionic and basic** (Na₂O gives NaOH, pH 13–14; MgO, pH about 9); Al₂O₃ is **amphoteric**; SiO₂ is **giant covalent and acidic** (reacts with hot concentrated NaOH, not with water); P₄O₁₀ and SO₂/SO₃ are **covalent and acidic** (giving H₃PO₄ and H₂SO₃/H₂SO₄). **Chlorides**: NaCl and MgCl₂ are ionic and dissolve to give neutral or slightly acidic solutions; AlCl₃, SiCl₄ and PCl₅ are covalent and **hydrolyse** in water, giving fumes of HCl and acidic solutions.',
  ], 'periodic-table', 'Link every Period 3 trend to structure and bonding: metallic → giant covalent → simple molecular.',
  ['SiCl₄ reacts with water to give:', ['SiO₂ and HCl, with steamy fumes', 'Si and Cl₂', 'A neutral solution of SiCl₄', 'No reaction'], 'SiCl₄ + 2H₂O → SiO₂ + 4HCl.'],
  [['Periodicity', 'The repeating pattern of properties across the periods.'], ['Amphoteric', 'Able to react with both acids and bases.'], ['Metalloid', 'An element with properties between those of metals and non-metals.']],
  [
    { q: 'Write equations for the reactions of Al₂O₃ with HCl and with NaOH, and explain what they show.', steps: ['With acid: **Al₂O₃ + 6HCl → 2AlCl₃ + 3H₂O** (the oxide acts as a base).', 'With alkali: **Al₂O₃ + 2NaOH + 3H₂O → 2NaAl(OH)₄** (the oxide acts as an acid, forming sodium aluminate).', 'Reacting with both shows that aluminium oxide is **amphoteric**.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-sblock-1', 'ac-periodicity', '6.2', 'The s-block elements: Groups I and II', 14, ['s-block', 'Group I', 'Group II'], [
    'The s-block metals have one (Group I) or two (Group II) outer electrons, which they lose easily to form M⁺ or M²⁺ ions. Down each group, atomic and ionic radii increase, ionisation energies fall and **reactivity increases**. They are strong reducing agents and form ionic compounds.',
    'Reactions: with **water**, Group I metals give hydroxides and hydrogen (Li steadily, Na rapidly, K with a lilac flame, Rb and Cs explosively); Group II metals react more slowly: Mg reacts with steam to give MgO + H₂, Ca reacts steadily with cold water. With **oxygen**, Li forms Li₂O, Na mainly Na₂O₂ (peroxide), and K, Rb and Cs form superoxides MO₂. They form ionic **hydrides** such as NaH, containing the H⁻ ion. **Flame tests**: Li red, Na yellow, K lilac, Ca brick red, Sr crimson, Ba apple green.',
    '**Solubility trends** in Group II: hydroxides become **more soluble** down the group (Mg(OH)₂ is used as an antacid because it is only sparingly soluble), while sulphates become **less soluble** (BaSO₄ is so insoluble it is used for the sulphate test and in barium meals for X-rays). Lime (Ca(OH)₂) neutralises acidic soils and is used in building.',
    '**Thermal stability**: Group II carbonates and nitrates decompose on heating to the oxide (with CO₂, or with NO₂ and O₂), and they become **more stable down the group**. A small cation with a high charge density **polarises** the large carbonate or nitrate ion, weakening its bonds. Group I carbonates (except Li₂CO₃) do not decompose at Bunsen temperatures, and Group I nitrates (except LiNO₃) give the nitrite and oxygen. Lithium resembles magnesium (a **diagonal relationship**) because Li⁺ and Mg²⁺ have similar charge densities.',
  ], 'flame-test', 'For thermal stability and solubility trends, explain with **charge density** and **polarisation**, not just "it goes down the group".',
  ['Which decomposes at the lowest temperature?', ['MgCO₃', 'CaCO₃', 'SrCO₃', 'BaCO₃'], 'Mg²⁺ is the smallest, so it polarises the carbonate ion most.'],
  [['Charge density', 'The charge of an ion divided by its size; high for small, highly charged ions.'], ['Diagonal relationship', 'The similarity between Li and Mg, Be and Al, due to similar charge densities.'], ['Thermal decomposition', 'The breakdown of a compound by heat.']],
  [
    { q: 'Write equations for heating (a) calcium carbonate, (b) magnesium nitrate, (c) sodium nitrate.', steps: ['(a) **CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂**.', '(b) **2Mg(NO₃)₂ → 2MgO + 4NO₂ + O₂**: brown fumes of NO₂ are seen.', '(c) **2NaNO₃ → 2NaNO₂ + O₂**: no brown fumes; the gas relights a glowing splint.'] },
  ]),

  // ---------- 7. Phase equilibria ----------
  L('ac-phase-1', 'ac-phase', '7.1', 'Vapour pressure, phase diagrams and Raoult’s law', 14, ['Phase equilibria'], [
    'In a closed container, molecules escape from a liquid and return to it until the rates are equal: the pressure of the vapour at this equilibrium is the **saturated vapour pressure**. It rises with temperature. A liquid **boils** when its vapour pressure equals the external pressure, which is why water boils below 100 °C high on Mount Cameroon.',
    'A **phase diagram** shows which phase is stable at each temperature and pressure. For water, lines divide the solid, liquid and vapour regions; they meet at the **triple point** (0.01 °C, 0.6 kPa), where all three phases coexist. The solid-liquid line slopes slightly to the **left** because ice is less dense than water: increasing the pressure melts ice. The liquid-vapour line ends at the **critical point**, above which liquid and gas cannot be distinguished.',
    '**Raoult’s law**: for an ideal mixture of liquids A and B, the partial vapour pressure of each component equals its **mole fraction in the liquid** times the vapour pressure of the pure component: **p_A = x_A p°_A**. The total vapour pressure is p_A + p_B. **Ideal mixtures** form when the forces between unlike molecules are similar to those between like molecules (hexane and heptane, benzene and methylbenzene): there is no heat change and no volume change on mixing.',
    'The vapour above an ideal mixture is **richer in the more volatile component**. On a **boiling point-composition diagram**, the vapour curve lies above the liquid curve. Each boil-and-condense step moves the composition towards the more volatile component; a **fractionating column** performs many such steps, so the more volatile liquid distils first.',
  ], 'boiling-composition', 'Mole fraction x_A = n_A ÷ (n_A + n_B). Mole fractions in the vapour are found from the partial pressures: y_A = p_A ÷ p_total.',
  ['At constant temperature, what happens to the vapour pressure of a liquid if more liquid is added to the closed container?', ['It stays the same', 'It doubles', 'It halves', 'It falls to zero'], 'Saturated vapour pressure depends only on the liquid and the temperature.'],
  [['Saturated vapour pressure', 'The pressure of a vapour in equilibrium with its liquid at a given temperature.'], ['Triple point', 'The temperature and pressure at which solid, liquid and vapour coexist.'], ['Raoult’s law', 'p_A = x_A × p°_A for each component of an ideal mixture.']],
  [
    { q: 'At 298 K, p°(benzene) = 12.7 kPa and p°(methylbenzene) = 3.8 kPa. Find the total vapour pressure of a mixture of 1 mol benzene and 3 mol methylbenzene, and the mole fraction of benzene in the vapour.', steps: ['x(benzene) = 1 ÷ 4 = 0.25; x(methylbenzene) = 0.75.', 'p(benzene) = 0.25 × 12.7 = 3.18 kPa; p(methylbenzene) = 0.75 × 3.8 = 2.85 kPa.', 'Total = **6.03 kPa**.', 'In the vapour, y(benzene) = 3.18 ÷ 6.03 = **0.53**: richer in benzene (0.25 in the liquid), the more volatile liquid.'] },
  ]),

  L('ac-phase-2', 'ac-phase', '7.2', 'Non-ideal mixtures, azeotropes, steam distillation and partition', 14, ['Phase equilibria', 'Separation'], [
    'Many mixtures **deviate** from Raoult’s law. In a **positive deviation**, the attractions between unlike molecules are **weaker** than between like molecules (ethanol and water, ethanol and cyclohexane): molecules escape more easily, the vapour pressure is **higher** than predicted, mixing is endothermic and the volume increases. A large positive deviation produces a maximum in vapour pressure and a **minimum-boiling azeotrope**.',
    'In a **negative deviation**, unlike molecules attract **more strongly**, often through hydrogen bonding (trichloromethane and propanone, nitric acid and water): the vapour pressure is **lower**, mixing is exothermic and a **maximum-boiling azeotrope** can form. An **azeotrope** boils at constant temperature with vapour of the same composition as the liquid, so fractional distillation cannot separate it. Ethanol and water form an azeotrope of about 96% ethanol (boiling at 78.2 °C); other methods, such as adding a third component or using a drying agent, are needed to obtain pure ethanol.',
    '**Immiscible liquids** each exert their full vapour pressure independently, so the mixture boils when **p°_A + p°_B = atmospheric pressure**, below the boiling point of either liquid. **Steam distillation** uses this to distil heat-sensitive or high-boiling substances (essential oils such as eucalyptus and lemongrass oil, phenylamine) at below 100 °C. The mass ratio in the distillate is **m_A ÷ m_B = (p°_A M_A) ÷ (p°_B M_B)**.',
    'The **partition law**: when a solute distributes itself between two immiscible solvents at constant temperature, the ratio of its concentrations is constant: **K = [X] in solvent 1 ÷ [X] in solvent 2**, provided the solute is in the same molecular form in both. **Solvent extraction** uses this; extracting with **several small portions** removes more solute than one large portion of the same total volume.',
  ], 'distillation', 'Explain deviations from Raoult’s law in terms of the **forces between unlike molecules** compared with like molecules.',
  ['A mixture of propanone and trichloromethane shows a negative deviation because:', ['Hydrogen bonds form between the unlike molecules', 'The unlike molecules repel', 'It is an ideal mixture', 'The molecules are non-polar'], 'Stronger attractions lower the vapour pressure and give off heat on mixing.'],
  [['Azeotrope', 'A mixture that boils at constant temperature with vapour of the same composition as the liquid.'], ['Steam distillation', 'Distilling a water-immiscible substance with steam so it boils below 100 °C.'], ['Partition coefficient', 'The constant ratio of a solute’s concentrations in two immiscible solvents.']],
  [
    { q: 'K for iodine between trichloromethane and water is 85. 0.10 g of iodine is in 100 cm³ of water. How much is extracted by (a) 20 cm³ of trichloromethane at once, (b) two 10 cm³ portions?', steps: ['(a) Let x g dissolve in the 20 cm³ of trichloromethane: (x ÷ 20) ÷ ((0.10 − x) ÷ 100) = 85.', 'So 100x = 1700(0.10 − x), giving 1800x = 170 and x = **0.0944 g** extracted.', '(b) First 10 cm³: 100x = 850(0.10 − x), so 950x = 85 and x = 0.0895 g; 0.0105 g remains in the water.', 'Second 10 cm³: 950y = 850 × 0.0105, so y = 0.0094 g. Total = **0.0989 g**, more than with one 20 cm³ portion.'] },
  ]),
];
